> I believe the only way to keep the original names is by building.... Oh, Jay
> beat me.

For built-in scsi drivers the order in which they are scanned, and
assigned names, depends on how they were compiled into a kernel, I
believe, although for a general PCI bus scan there is an option
"reverse", or someting close, which you can pass to kernel.

You may, of course, load your drivers (or some of them - like those not
used by your boot disk) as modules then you may control a loading and
scanning order yourself - in initrd if required.  You can also make this
trouble manageable if you will mount file systems by a label, or uuid,
and not by a device name.  Your root device as passed to your boot image
may still "float" depending on a kernel, and you cannot so far identify
swap by a label, but this is minor as trying to mount wrong swap device
will simply fail so you may attempt all of them.  All other "labelable"
file systems will find itself automagically.

   Michal




