I'm not sure if this is all wishful <italic>thinking</italic> or not.
Fact is, when released, the products will say Itanium on the outside, but
should say "Alpha Inside". This is pretty obvious if you've had a chance
to speak with any of the engineers involved in the transition. 


Intel did not purchase the technology to eliminate competition. It was
purchased because Alpha has many years and a great deal of money already
behind the advanced research and engineering. Alpha also has nearly 10
years of unsurpassed/stable performance behind it. Sounds reasonably
compelling, especially if you are a company who, for quite some time, has
been telling the world you'll be providing CPU technology that will offer
the best performance in the industry.


Moving the intellectual properties to Intel should satisfy the two major
short falls many have pointed a finger at for years; the fact that Alpha
(although superior in almost every performance category) was not a
"mainstream" architecture, and not volume priced.


All those who continued to pay an equal price, if not higher, for
performance based systems from other manufactures, should now feel some
degree of vindication for not having taken a leap to Alpha technology.
While Alpha has held price/performance leadership over other products for
nearly 10 years, it was never considered mainstream, so such a move would
have required some degree of risk (no pun) taking and perhaps a dash of
conviction in ones own ability to drive educated decision making within
an organization. Those who waited will have a new choice in "mainstream"
engineering, and if Intel cannot offer the products at volume pricing,
then perhaps all the claims about Digital and Compaq lining their pockets
may have been less than accurate.


Alpha has survived since 1992, and although repackaged, it looks as
though we'll continue to see the benefits of the investment in this
technology for another 10 years. This may not come as good news to some.
I happen to feel this spells goodness for those who require high end
computing products to accomplish their research. If you're waiting for
Alpha to die, check back in 10 years.


cheers

~m


