> > I created a nsd disklabel (partition c only) taalking default values and
> > switching type to 8 (ext2). Then I rebooted. The disk was recognized by
> > the kernel and the partition as hda3. I used mke2fs to format the disk
> > and (unfortunately) moved data from the old disk to the new one. After
> > the next reboot however the disk was no longer accessible with the
> > message 'corrupt partition table'.
> >
> > What happened? What can I do to repair the partition table? How can I
> > get my data back??

My theory is that you made a "c" partition that covers the whole
disk and tried to _USE_ it as a partition.  Don't do that.

The whole-disk "c" partition is for Tru64 alias DU alias ...
For Linux, you should never use the whole-disk partition.  In
fact, if doing just Linux, you don't need a whole-disk partition.
However, if you have one, you must mark it unused and actually
use the other partitions.

Good luck.

Robert Riches
richesr1@inetarena.com

